{
  "dom_id": "step-q53031",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224458/assets/fe3724e266b68939b97f07fd7f428173ff0693b0d309d085187e44a45ba466ac.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a kite?",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224458/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a trapezoid. It doesn't have two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, therefore, it is not a kite.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a rectangle. Since it doesn't have two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, it is not a kite.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Therefore, it has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a parallelogram. Any two adjacent sides are not congruent with each other, therefore, it is not a kite.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a kite.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
